{:edb/db-before-t 864 :edb/db-after-t 865 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534177 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:27:37.394Z" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413193 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413199 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413203 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413190 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413196 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413201 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413202 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413194 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413197 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413198 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413204 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413189 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413191 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413192 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413195 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129294 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413200 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129368 1050 840.0 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126731 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186129539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413177 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129539 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses $m_{1} = 0.5kg$, $m_{2} = 2kg$, and $m_{3} = 1kg$ located at $x_{1} = 2m$, $x_{2} = 3m$, and $x_{3} = 5m$, respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186129539 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses ${m}_{1}=0.5\\,\\text{kg},$ ${m}_{2}=2\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${m}_{3}=1\\,\\text{kg}$ located at ${x}_{1}=2\\,\\text{m},$ ${x}_{2}=3\\,\\text{m},$ and ${x}_{3}=5\\,\\text{m},$ respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129539 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =0.5, & \\,{m}_{2} & =2, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3, & \\,{x}_{3} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =0.5\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1+6+5 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126737 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186129540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413170 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129540 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =5, & \\,{m}_{2} & =1, & \\,{m}_{3} & =4, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3, & \\,{x}_{3} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10+3+20 \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $33\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126743 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186129541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413169 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129541 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses $m_{1} = 2kg$, $m_{2} = 5kg$, and $m_{3} = 7kg$ located at $x_{1} = 1.5$, $x_{2} = 5$, and $x_{3} = 10$, respectively, along the $x$-axis, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?" 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186129541 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses ${m}_{1}=2\\,\\text{kg},$ ${m}_{2}=5\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${m}_{3}=7\\,\\text{kg}$ located at ${x}_{1}=1.5,$ ${x}_{2}=5,$ and ${x}_{3}=10,$ respectively, along the $x$-axis, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129541 1074 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =5, & \\,{m}_{3} & =7, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.5, & \\,{x}_{2} & =5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+5+7 \\\\  & =14\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1.5+5\\cdot 5+7\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =3+25+70 \\\\  & =98\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{98}{14}=7.\n$$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126755 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186129543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413174 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129543 1071 "![](images/46/467a702d26a9e8c088a74aca889e2adbc68c8f86192b7138b37bdbdff09a7582.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above, where the distances along the $x$ and $y$ axes are measured in meters. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186129543 1071 "![](images/46/467a702d26a9e8c088a74aca889e2adbc68c8f86192b7138b37bdbdff09a7582.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above, where the distances along the $x$ and $y$ axes are measured in meters. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(x^(―), y^(―))$, what is the value of $x^(―)$?" 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186129543 1074 "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1,\\,{m}_{4}=5.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2-6+1-15 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=-\\frac{18}{11}.\n$$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186130475 1064 "Suppose we have two point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $20\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and $40\\,\\textrm{kg},$ respectively, connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0948ebde55c4a5f8819f741e70a44c31fca75218a65d1f88d503367f213c9e31.png)\n\nOur intuition tells us that if we place a **fulcrum** (i.e., a balancing point) at the *midpoint* between the two masses, the rod will tip down at the right end, as shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93e4ca7726e4c01793440e23823622d19ec284104e5a062af40a6b35407d410d.png)\n\nTo balance the rod perfectly, we must move the fulcrum closer to the larger mass. \n\n![](images/00/00fb578163a19566869582929dfe9dc351ed3b75c44221f8d3e68157f40f08f7.png)\n\nAt what distance from the first mass ($d_1$ in the picture) should the fulcrum be placed for the masses to balance perfectly?\n\nTo answer this, we must introduce the concept of a **moment**:\n\n*The moment of a mass about a point $P$ equals the product of the mass and its displacement from $P.$*\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Loosely speaking, moment describes the amount of *turning effect* produced by a mass about a point (usually the tip of the fulcrum).\n\n- For masses measured in kilograms and distances in meters, the units of a moment are $\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm m.$\n\n- Moment is a *vector* quantity. A moment is either *positive* (representing *clockwise* induced motion) or *negative* (for *counterclockwise* motion). A moment's sign (positive/clockwise or negative/counterclockwise) is determined by the direction the rod would rotate if the corresponding mass were the only mass in the system.\n\nLet's now see how to use moments to calculate the required distance $d_1.$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186130475 1064 "Suppose we have two point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $20\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and $40\\,\\textrm{kg},$ respectively, connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0948ebde55c4a5f8819f741e70a44c31fca75218a65d1f88d503367f213c9e31.png)\n\nOur intuition tells us that if we place a **fulcrum** (i.e., a balancing point) at the *midpoint* between the two masses, the rod will tip down at the right end, as shown below.\n\nHow do we find their center of mass, [math]\\overline{x}?[/math]\n\n![](images/93/93e4ca7726e4c01793440e23823622d19ec284104e5a062af40a6b35407d410d.png)\n\nA typical example is a child's seesaw. If one child sits at each end of the seesaw with the fulcrum halfway between, the heavier child falls, and the lighter child rises.\n\nTo balance the rod perfectly, we must move the fulcrum closer to the larger mass. \n\nThe center of mass [math]\\overline{x}[/math] is where the fulcrum should be to balance the seesaw.\n\n![](images/00/00fb578163a19566869582929dfe9dc351ed3b75c44221f8d3e68157f40f08f7.png)\n\nAt what distance from the first mass ($d_1$ in the picture) should the fulcrum be placed for the masses to balance perfectly?\n\nTo answer this, we must introduce the concept of a **moment**:\n\n> *The moment of a mass about a point $P$ equals the product of the mass and its displacement from $P.$*\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Loosely speaking, moment describes the amount of *turning effect* produced by a mass about a point (usually the tip of the fulcrum).\n\n- For masses measured in kilograms and distances in meters, the units of a moment are $\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm m.$\n\n- Moment is a *vector* quantity. A moment is either *positive* (representing *clockwise* induced motion) or *negative* (for *counterclockwise* motion). A moment's sign (positive/clockwise or negative/counterclockwise) is determined by the direction the rod would rotate if the corresponding mass were the only mass in the system.\n\nLet's now see how to use moments to calculate the required distance $d_1.$\n\n* Imagine you're tightening a nut with a spanner. It's possible to produce the same turning effect with a long spanner of smaller mass as with a shorter planner with a more heavy mass." 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186130478 1064 "Suppose we have a collection of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nThe system's **center of mass** is the point $\\bar{x}$ on the $x$-axis where the system's total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment as the original system. \n\n![](images/e2/e2d7dcb177010f0105d7887e14b84e52527381caa17ca01b91027db6d6cd40a7.png)\n\n \n\nLet's derive the equation for the center of mass $\\bar{x}$ for this system.\n\nThe total moment of the distributed masses about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\nm = m_1+m_2+\\cdots m_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i ,\n$$\n\nthen the total moment about the origin of the single mass $m$ located at $\\bar{x}$ equals\n\n$$\nm \\bar{x}.\n$$\n\nEquating our two moments, we get\n\n$$\nM_O = m \\bar{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\bar{x}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\bar{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe center of mass is an important physical quantity, as it gives the location where a fulcrum would need to be placed for the system to balance perfectly." 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186130478 1064 "Suppose we have a collection of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nThe system's **center of mass** is the point $\\bar{x}$ on the $x$-axis where the system's total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment as the original system. \n\n![](images/e2/e2d7dcb177010f0105d7887e14b84e52527381caa17ca01b91027db6d6cd40a7.png)\n\nLet's derive the equation for the center of mass $\\bar{x}$ for this system.\n\nThe total moment of the distributed masses about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\nm = m_1+m_2+\\cdots m_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i ,\n$$\n\nthen the total moment about the origin of the single mass $m$ located at $\\bar{x}$ equals\n\n$$\nm \\bar{x}.\n$$\n\nEquating our two moments, we get\n\n$$\nM_O = m \\bar{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\bar{x}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\bar{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe center of mass is an important physical quantity, as it gives the location where a fulcrum would need to be placed for the system to balance perfectly." 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186130479 1064 "When a collection of masses is distributed in two or more dimensions of space, we must take moments about a fixed **axis.**\n\nIn the following work, we will only consider moments about the coordinate axes. \n\nConsider the collection of point masses below, where the distances along the $x$- and $y$-axes are measured in meters. \n\n![](images/32/32f9c207b835469dba729e3d5745dd1225eed6153aac4517e2c40e61b7edb126.png)\n\nThe center of mass of this two-dimensional system, denoted $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ is the point where the entire mass of the system could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the $x$- and $y$-axes as the original system. \n\nMore precisely, the center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ in the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's compute $\\bar{x},$ the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system. \n\nFirst, we denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of these masses are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -2, \\qquad x_2 = -1, \\qquad x_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $\\boldsymbol y$**-axis** is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6-5+16 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_y}{m} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186130479 1064 "When a collection of masses is distributed in two or more dimensions of space, we must take moments about a fixed **axis.**\n\nIn the following work, we will only consider moments about the coordinate axes. \n\nConsider the collection of point masses below, where the distances along the $x$- and $y$-axes are measured in meters. \n\n![](images/32/32f9c207b835469dba729e3d5745dd1225eed6153aac4517e2c40e61b7edb126.png)\n\nThe center of mass of this two-dimensional system, denoted $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ is the point where the entire mass of the system could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the $x$- and $y$-axes as the original system. \n\nMore precisely, the center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ in the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's compute $\\bar{x},$ the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system. \n\nSince we require the [math]x[/math]-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the [math]y[/math]-axis.\n\nFirst, we denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of these masses are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -2, \\qquad x_2 = -1, \\qquad x_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $\\boldsymbol y$**-axis** is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6-5+16 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_y}{m} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$" 13194139534177 17592186045417 false] [17592186413069 1080 "\\frac{16}{7}" 13194139534177 17592186045417 true] [17592186413